  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Alessandra was the youngest of Serafino Ferruzzi’s four children. Before his death, the founder transferred the family companies to them, giving each daughter a substantial share. The heirs then delegated operating authority to Raul Gardini, husband of Alessandra’s sister Idina, as Ferruzzi grew from agricultural commodities into sugar, chemicals and the Montedison industrial group.\n\nThe expansion created extraordinary scale but also large debts and deep family disagreements. Banking intervention ended the heirs’ control, while the wider Tangentopoli investigations engulfed executives and culminated in Gardini’s death. Alessandra largely withdrew from public life and later described the group as surviving principally in memory. Her experience is one of inherited ownership followed by the dissolution of a celebrated industrial dynasty.",
